# Volatile session files — regenerated each session
.tool-counter
.prev-token-reading
session.yaml
focus.yaml
# T-222: pattern was `tier0-approval` (no leading dot) — never matched the real
# `.tier0-approval` token, so transient Tier-0 grants leaked into commits. Glob the
# `.pending`/`.consumed` siblings too.
.tier0-approval*
# T-410: the Watchtower session signing key — the value that signs fw_session_<port>
# and the CSRF token inside it. It is regenerated on demand by _resolve_secret_key()
# (web/app.py), so it is volatile in exactly the sense this file is for; it was never
# listed, and 371 tracked files under .context/working/ meant it was swept in without
# anyone choosing it. Second instance of the T-222 shape directly above: an ignore file
# that is trusted to cover a directory while the one entry that matters is absent.
.fw-secret-key

# T-417: THIRD instance of the shape the two entries above describe. T-413 dumped the whole
# rail topic here (1.6 MB, every message body of a 464-message conversation) so the base64
# fixture payloads would not have to pass through the agent's context — a good call about
# context, made in the wrong directory. A checkpoint handover's bulk add published it four
# minutes later, into a repo that mirrors to GitHub. Not secret; simply not chosen, and
# regenerable from the hub at any time.
#
# The lesson is not "add another line". It is that under this directory INCLUSION IS THE
# DEFAULT, so every new working file is one bulk `git add` from publication. Regenerable
# scratch belongs in the session scratchpad, outside the repo. These two entries cover the
# artifacts that P-011 verification lines must write inside the tree, where the scratchpad's
# session-specific path is not available to them.
t413/
*-probe.out
